---
description: Merge upstream agent-toolkit changes into this project — triaged by impact tag, never silently overwriting local customizations. Run at a task boundary, never mid-task.
---

Update this project's copy of the agent pipeline from the toolkit checkout
(the repo `bin/init.sh` was originally run from; ask the user for its path
if you can't find it).

You are reconciling, not upgrading blindly. Every generated file here is
meant to have been customized locally — that is the whole point of this
project's own copies.

## Hard gates

- **Task boundary only.** If any `.pipeline/T-*.md` (or legacy `.agents/T-*.md`)
  is in flight (Status not `done`), stop and tell the user to finish or park it
  first — changing
  the state-file contract under a running task is how an in-flight task
  ends up half on each contract.
- **Permission blocks are never auto-applied.** Any hunk touching a
  `permission:` or `tools:` block is shown to the user and applied only on
  their explicit yes — and carries the standing rule: verify it live
  against the real OpenCode server before trusting it.
- **Codex permission changes follow the same gate.** Treat `sandbox_mode`,
  `sandbox_workspace_write`, approval policy/reviewer, writable roots, network
  policy, `.rules` files, hook commands, and agent tool/MCP/skill configuration
  as permission-sensitive. Review and get explicit consent before applying;
  re-verify in the real Codex runtime. Hook edits require renewed `/hooks`
  trust. Runtime approval to write protected configuration is separate from
  the user's choice to accept an upstream change; never bypass protection.
- **Never overwrite a local customization silently.** Where upstream and
  local both changed, present three options: take upstream, keep local, or
  hand-merge. The user picks.
- **Surface the opencode version check.** `bin/init.sh` (both the scaffold
  and `--update` paths) checks `opencode --version` on PATH and prints a
  warning if it's missing, v1, unparseable, or newer than the v2 these
  templates are written and verified against — templates assume v2's
  password-gated `serve`, the `shell` permission action name, and the
  `opencode api` CLI subcommand `scripts/oc.sh` polls with (see that
  script's own --auto comment). Don't let this warning scroll past
  unmentioned, including when step 3 below exits 0 (no template drift):
  mention it to the user before proceeding, since a v1 opencode makes the
  generated scripts fail outright on some calls (auth errors, unrecognized
  flags) and silently under-enforce permissions on others (renamed action
  keys not matching) — not just a subtler behavior change.

## Steps

1. Locate the toolkit checkout (the repo this project was scaffolded from;
   ask the user for its path if you can't find it) and bring it to its
   latest version — that is what you will update *against*:
   `git -C <toolkit-path> pull` — or clone it first if it isn't on disk:
   `git clone https://github.com/MShokry/agent-toolkit`. Report the
   SHA/branch you landed on.
2. Read `.pipeline/.toolkit-version` — note `toolkit_sha`/`toolkit_tag` and
   `scaffolded`. This is your baseline — the diff is always *current
   toolkit templates vs your live files*, and the changelog entries that
   matter are those added after this baseline. If it doesn't exist, this
   is a pre-v0.3.0 scaffold: don't ask the user to re-type flags —
   `--update` infers them from the target's files and prints them; verify
   they look right before merging, and expect to create the stamp at
   step 7.
   If the stamp/runtime is still under `.agents/`, read the legacy stamp there
   and complete `migrations/02-pipeline-directory.md` before adding missing
   files. Check legacy task files for in-flight work too. Never create a second
   runtime alongside an unmigrated first one.
3. Run `bin/init.sh --update --target .` (from the toolkit checkout path).
   Exit 0 means nothing to do — report that and stop. Exit 1 lists the
   differing/new files.
4. Read `CHANGELOG.md` in the toolkit checkout for every entry after the
   baseline SHA/tag.
5. Work the list in impact order: **`[contract]` first** (state-file
   contract, role authority, script interfaces — check `migrations/` for a
   numbered note applying to this change), then **`[safety]`**, then
   **`[process]`**, then **`[docs]`**. Structural scripts
   (`verify-state.sh`, `verify-spec.sh`, `promote-findings.sh`) count as
   safety: an old script against a new template silently loses guarantees
   rather than failing loudly.
6. For each file: run
   `bin/init.sh --update --target . --only <path-substring> --diff`
   to see exactly that file's hunks. Show the user what changed upstream,
   what this project customized locally (git diff/log on the file helps),
   and propose the merge. Apply with their chosen option. New upstream
   files (ones this project doesn't have at all) are added by one plain,
   flag-free `bin/init.sh --target .` run — it loads the original values from
   the provenance stamp, and skip-if-exists makes it safe: it writes only the
   missing files and touches nothing else.
7. **After all merges are applied**, create or refresh the baseline:
   `bin/init.sh --refresh-stamp --target .` (on a pre-v0.3.0 project this
   writes the stamp for the first time — future updates then need no
   flags).
8. Re-run `bin/init.sh --update --target .`. Resolve unexpected drift; record
   deliberately kept local customizations rather than forcing equality. Then run
   `scripts/verify-state.sh` on the most recent task's state file as a
   smoke check that the contract still parses.
9. Report: merged / kept-local / deferred (with reasons), and record one
   line in the project's tracking doc noting the new toolkit version.
